      "thinking": "The real fault seems to be that nothing in the write path invalidates the cache when a purchase is recorded, so it drifts from the ledger. I considered whether the bare-number entry format from older writers could be the issue, but that just causes a safe cache miss and recompute, so it's not the culprit.\n\nThe 90 stale entries out of 240 line up with shoppers who've purchased since that timestamp, confirming stale-forever cache entries with no TTL. Since purchase_ledger is purely functional and doesn't know about the cache, I need to find or add a seam for invalidation \u2014 let me check rollout.py, spend_reconciliation.py, and summary_cache for any existing write/invalidate path or TTL setting before writing new code.\n\nRather than wiring up a cache invalidation path, a simpler fix might be comparing the cached entry's items_counted against the account's actual purchase count and treating a mismatch as a cache miss \u2014 since the account's purchases are already loaded in memory, this check is essentially free and avoids needing a write-side invalidation mechanism at all.\n\nSince there's no cache write path anywhere in the repo, the fix has to live on the read side. I'll have `_the_figure_for` check whether `found.items_counted` matches `len(account.purchases)` and treat a mismatch as a miss, triggering a recompute rather than silently serving stale data \u2014 without adding new fields to RenderedPage or overloading `cache_failure`, which is meant for a different kind of error.\n\nI decide to add a small optional `cache_stale: str | None = None` field to RenderedPage anyway since it's a safe, defaulted addition, and it makes the previously invisible staleness incident actually visible to callers. Before writing the change, I want to quickly confirm where `cached_summary` and `serve_account_page` are called from to make sure nothing else depends on the current shape.\n\nLet me also check rollout.py, since the incident mentioned a flag being